"""Thirty five-slide study and life routines for Her Better Hours."""


def parse(text: str) -> list[dict]:
    posts = []
    for row in text.strip().splitlines():
        parts = [part.strip() for part in row.split("|")]
        if len(parts) != 5:
            raise ValueError(f"Expected a cover and four steps: {row}")
        steps = []
        for part in parts[1:]:
            title, explanation = [piece.strip() for piece in part.split("~", 1)]
            steps.append({"title": title, "explanation": explanation})
        posts.append({"hook": parts[0], "steps": steps})
    if len(posts) != 30:
        raise ValueError(f"Expected 30 posts, got {len(posts)}")
    return posts


HER = parse("""
how to study after class when you're already tired | don't go home first~go to the library while you're already out. the couch is too hard to leave. | keep it to one thing~one chapter or one problem set. you're not catching up on everything today. | close your notes for a minute~write down what you remember, then look at what you missed. | write down where you stopped~tomorrow you can pick up there instead of reading everything again.
a study block before noon without a 5am alarm | write tomorrow's task down before bed~one less thing to decide when you're half asleep. | put your phone in your bag~you can check it when the first block is over. | set a 25-minute timer~it's a lot easier to sit down for 25 minutes than for "the whole morning." | see what you remember~close your notes and write a few things down before you move on.
the night routine when tomorrow is already busy | find out when you need to leave~check the actual time, including the trip there. | put your bag by the door~charger, keys, notebook. whatever you always end up looking for. | wear the outfit you know works~don't make tomorrow morning a fashion emergency. | write down the first thing to do~one line is enough. you can figure out the rest tomorrow.
how to actually get work done at the library | know why you're going~"finish the problem set" is better than "study for a while." | open the assignment first~don't spend 30 minutes finding the perfect seat. | take a break away from your screen~walk around or get food. scrolling at the desk doesn't feel like a break. | write down where you stopped~future you shouldn't have to retrace the whole afternoon.
if you've read the same paragraph five times | stop reading for a second~try to say what it means without looking at the page. | write down the part you lost~now you know what you actually need to go back to. | check that part of your notes~you don't have to restart the whole chapter. | explain it one more time~pretend you're telling a friend who missed class.
the 30-minute reset for a morning that's going badly | get out of pajamas~you don't need a whole routine. just get dressed. | move the stuff in your way~clear enough room to open your laptop or notebook. | pick one small thing to finish~an email, a page, a few questions. something with an end. | give it 20 minutes~you can decide what happens next when the timer ends.
when every class suddenly has something due | put the dates in one place~check the syllabus and the class site, then write them all down. | look at this week first~the assignment due friday matters more than the one due next month. | open the one you're avoiding~even an outline makes it less mysterious. | leave a note when you switch~write the next step before you close the tab.
a sunday reset that doesn't take all sunday | check monday morning~what time do you have to be somewhere? | pack the annoying little things~keys, charger, notebook. the stuff that slows you down. | do one chore for monday~laundry or groceries, whatever you'll actually need. | stop there~you don't have to spend your entire day off preparing to work.
the early-class routine for people who hate mornings | put your alarm where you have to get up~give yourself one less chance to snooze for 40 minutes. | keep breakfast simple~make something you can manage half asleep. | check your bag before you leave~especially the thing you need for that class. | get there a little early~five calm minutes beats running in as it starts.
how to study when you can't focus on anything | write down what's distracting you~get it on paper so you don't keep trying to remember it. | pick one page or one question~don't stare at the entire course at once. | start anywhere you can~you don't need to find the perfect order. | take a real break~get up from the desk, then come back to the next question.
the night before an assignment is due | check what you actually have to turn in~read the instructions one more time. | finish the missing pieces first~a complete draft is more useful than one perfect paragraph. | check the file and deadline~wrong format and wrong time are avoidable headaches. | submit before you get in bed~don't leave the upload for five minutes before it's due.
how to make a cafe study session worth it | show up with a task~the coffee shop isn't going to tell you what to work on. | download your files first~wifi always seems to fail when you finally sit down. | do one block before your phone comes out~give the first 25 minutes to the work you came for. | leave when you're done~staying longer doesn't make the work better.
five minutes tonight to make tomorrow less annoying | look at your first plan~know when you have to leave or log in. | find your keys and charger~put them where you'll grab them in the morning. | write down one task~make it specific enough that you can just start. | go to bed~you don't need to plan your entire life at 11pm.
getting back into studying after a few days off | open the last thing you worked on~you don't need to reread the whole unit. | try one question~see what you remember before going back through notes. | look up what you missed~spend ten minutes on that part. | put another session on your calendar~make it short enough that you'll actually do it.
if your study plan is longer than your studying | stop making a new schedule~use the one you already have for today. | pick the next due thing~a real deadline is a good place to start. | work for 15 minutes~no new app, no color coding, just the task. | plan the next block afterward~you'll know more once you've actually started.
studying after work when you're already exhausted | eat something first~don't open a textbook while you're waiting for dinner. | choose one short task~a few questions is enough for tonight. | put your phone somewhere else~one check can turn into your whole evening. | stop when you said you would~tomorrow still needs you awake.
if highlighting has become your whole study method | check what you're being tested on~use the syllabus or the questions your teacher gave you. | try a question without notes~see what you can answer right now. | go back to the parts you missed~that's where your time is useful. | try again later~give yourself another shot without the answer in front of you.
the morning routine for zero-motivation days | open the blinds~give the room a little daylight. | wash your face and get dressed~keep it basic. no need to reinvent yourself today. | finish one tiny task~pick something you can do in five minutes. | then choose what matters~you don't have to write off the whole day.
make your notes useful after class | turn each heading into a question~next week, you'll know what to try answering. | mark the confusing bit~don't cover it up with more highlighting. | write a two-line version~use words you'd actually say out loud. | come back tomorrow~see what you can explain without opening the notes.
the ten minutes between classes | put the last class away~close the tabs you don't need anymore. | check where you're going~don't spend the break looking for the room. | eat or drink if you need to~being hungry makes the next hour feel longer. | write down one question~bring it with you instead of forgetting it.
how to finish an assignment without overthinking it | read the rubric~find out what has to be there before you start. | make a rough outline~one line for each section is enough. | write the whole messy draft~fixing sentences is easier once the work exists. | check it against the rubric~make the missing fixes, then send it.
the 20-minute room reset before studying | take the dishes out~don't let them become the reason you avoid the desk. | put clothes in one pile~you can deal with laundry later. | clear one spot to work~you only need room for the stuff you're using. | stop cleaning~you came here to study, not reorganize your closet.
how to survive a week that's already packed | write down the fixed plans~class, shifts, appointments, all of it. | find the worst day~move anything flexible off that day. | do prep before the deadline~starting on the due date is a rough plan. | leave some empty space~something will run late. it always does.
studying in a loud house | find the quietest hour you've got~it doesn't have to be the same time every day. | take one task somewhere else~a library or cafe works for the thing that needs silence. | use headphones if they help~don't spend the whole session building the perfect setup. | save the harder reading for quiet~do easier questions when the house is busy.
what to do with a random hour between plans | pick something you can finish~not the project that needs your entire afternoon. | open it right away~the hour disappears fast if you spend 20 minutes settling in. | set an alarm for when to leave~give yourself time to get to the next place. | write down where you stopped~you'll be glad you did when you open it again.
a morning routine that starts at a normal hour | wake up with enough time to leave~you don't need a 4am alarm to have a good day. | get ready before you scroll~the phone can wait until you're dressed. | eat a breakfast you actually like~pick something you'd make on a regular weekday. | do one task when you arrive~start with the thing you already decided on.
practice questions without the panic spiral | do one without looking anything up~give yourself a chance to think first. | find the exact step you missed~"i don't get it" is too big to fix. | read the explanation~look for how they got from one step to the next. | try a similar question later~see if you can do it on your own.
ten minutes before you close your laptop | save what you'll need again~you don't need 25 tabs waiting for you tomorrow. | move unfinished tasks somewhere real~give them a date instead of keeping them in your head. | leave your first file ready~make tomorrow's start as easy as opening it. | close the laptop~you're allowed to be done for today.
how to have a decent day after sleeping in | check what time it is~make a plan for the day you have, not the one you missed. | pick one thing that matters~choose the task with the closest deadline. | work for a short block~starting at noon is still starting. | don't punish yourself with an all-nighter~move the rest and get some sleep.
a weekly study check-in that takes ten minutes | look at what's due next~put the dates in one place. | notice what you're avoiding~make that task small enough to open. | choose two times to study~put them where they fit your actual week. | leave one evening alone~you need time that isn't another task.
""")
